  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSoutherly flow will continue across the region today as we \nremain between high pressure off the Mid-Atlantic Coast and \nelongated low pressure extending from the Upper Midwest to the \nsouthern Plains. Warm advection will be ongoing with 925mb \ntemperatures peaking approximately 5C warmer than yesterday and \nbreezy south winds gusting to 20-25 mph in North Central and \nNorthwest Ohio. Aloft, 500mb heights of 582dm are above 90th \npercentiles values based on ILN sounding climatology for \nNovember 3rd. The anomalously warm airmass for early November \ncombined with clearing skies will result in high temperatures in\nthe low to mid 70s. Toledo has the best chance of tying or \nbreaking a record which is 77 degrees from 1987. Records for \nother northern Ohio sites can be found in the Climate portion of\nthe AFD below. Following the warm day, lows tonight will be \nmild ranging from the mid 50s to low 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA deep upper level trough extending to the southern \nPlains will pivot northeast towards the Great Lakes Region on \nWednesday. Favorable moisture transport will be focused towards Ohio \nand forcing will be provided by upper level jet dynamics as the \ntrough becomes negatively tilted aloft. Showers are expected to move \ninto Northwest Ohio Wednesday morning then expand eastward through \nWednesday night. Potential for thunderstorms is low but not zero. \nDid not include in the forecast with this cycle but can not rule out \nan isolated thunderstorm or two on Wednesday. South to southeast \nwinds will be elevated on Wednesday but the boundary layer is \nexpected to be stable enough to not bring down the stronger winds \nnoted with a 40-50 knot low level jet overhead in showers. In \ngeneral, most of the area is forecast to receive between a quarter \nand a half inch of rain. We will need to monitor convection across \nthe Lower Mississippi and Tennessee Valleys where moisture \ntransport northward could be disrupted by thunderstorm activity \nbut overall feel the high pops are justified ahead of the \noccluded front. \n\nTemperatures will still be warm on Wednesday ahead of the front. \nThursday is forecast to generally be dry as this trough lifts north \nacross Michigan and phases with another trough dropping south out of \nCanada. A well pronounced dry-slot aloft will result in dry \nconditions and clearing skies. Yet another trough is expected to \nmove out of the Four Corners Region into the Ohio Valley where it \nwill merge with the original trough. While model spread increases \nheading into the weekend, models generally support a cold front \nmoving south Friday bringing a chance of showers, especially \ndownwind of Lake Erie. Temperatures over the weekend will be near \nnormal values in the mid 50s but will feel cool compared to the warm \npattern we have been in.",
